  'Building Codes and Regulations',
  'Building Codes and Regulations',
  'Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and the authority having jurisdiction. The course covers I-Code navigation, occupancy and mixed use, construction type and allowable area, fire and life safety, means of egress, accessibility and universal design, public-health and MEP coordination, energy codes, zoning and site constraints, existing buildings, code-analysis documentation, permitting, and professional responsibility. A capstone applies code analysis to a design and produces a review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.',
  $bcdrjson${"slug": "building-codes-and-regulations", "title": "Building Codes and Regulations", "description": "Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and the authority having jurisdiction. The course covers I-Code navigation, occupancy and mixed use, construction type and allowable area, fire and life safety, means of egress, accessibility and universal design, public-health and MEP coordination, energy codes, zoning and site constraints, existing buildings, code-analysis documentation, permitting, and professional responsibility. A capstone applies code analysis to a design and produces a review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.", "topics": [{"id": "068d46b5-c594-4aba-8a7f-91dd593e4c3d", "type": "topic", "title": "U.S. Building Regulation Ecosystem: Public Safety, Model Codes, Adoption, AHJs, and Jurisdiction", "description": "Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through state or local adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and authority having jurisdiction.", "children": [{"id": "ce71d5e6-759d-4327-aebe-2d40c3db1d7e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Purpose and limits of codes", "children": [{"id": "fe12ede4-7a80-45aa-9c25-f7a23fb1cac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Life safety, health, accessibility, energy, resilience, and public welfare"}, {"id": "e31e2b1e-fbda-4d80-9fcd-14ea65f10866", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum compliance versus best practice"}, {"id": "106757df-7c0c-46ba-bd96-62e19980761a", "type": "concept", "title": "Code intent, prescriptive paths, performance paths, and equivalencies"}], "description": "Students learn that codes set minimum public-safety requirements, not the full measure of good design."}, {"id": "02f1c441-31a2-47cb-801f-223ebf715841", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Jurisdictional adoption", "children": [{"id": "2d905d28-b469-4c5c-8753-5417099d6e47", "type": "concept", "title": "State and municipal adoption cycles"}, {"id": "ddd57cba-cc0a-4499-b27e-883135edef42", "type": "concept", "title": "Local amendments, supplements, and administrative provisions"}, {"id": "9b636562-56f5-4a94-8c3e-94f1a8855f61", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority having jurisdiction, building official, fire marshal, zoning staff, and special reviewers"}], "description": "The point is to understand why the applicable code is local even when the model code is national."}, {"id": "ee5a6d36-0844-4a85-889c-b49fd866f971", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regulatory workflow", "children": [{"id": "9a08d723-2ec7-4e71-8e97-3d38ae9ae9df", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-application research, permit triggers, plan review, inspections, certificates of occupancy"}, {"id": "1cabced9-1c8e-4afd-ab82-cb3390529d69", "type": "concept", "title": "New construction, alterations, additions, changes of use, and tenant improvements"}, {"id": "29876e87-3de4-4125-a34c-b9205be449cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation, communication, and recordkeeping"}], "description": "Students see how code research becomes approvals."}]}, {"id": "de07ea01-3cd3-4e8e-ac73-f5e5abc7a60d", "type": "topic", "title": "International Codes Framework: IBC, IRC, IEBC, IFC, IECC, Referenced Standards, and Code Navigation", "description": "This module teaches students to navigate the I-Code family and understand how building, residential, existing building, fire, energy, mechanical, plumbing, structural, and referenced standards interact.", "children": [{"id": "2397f29c-5c95-4031-bcac-6488bf82e7e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "I-Code family", "children": [{"id": "a0a3818a-0850-4bd5-b652-6d92ee485e10", "type": "concept", "title": "IBC, IRC, IEBC, IFC, IECC, IMC, IPC, IFGC, IgCC, and zoning references"}, {"id": "992bb78d-26fd-4b71-92d9-8d0c320f1d11", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial versus residential scope"}, {"id": "6a1c642b-f0ba-433d-96d4-96ac73fd857e", "type": "concept", "title": "Building, fire, energy, mechanical, plumbing, and existing-building interfaces"}], "description": "Students learn which model code governs which kind of problem."}, {"id": "1e377454-4d59-4065-89da-831ceddd7c8e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Referenced standards", "children": [{"id": "f1a40993-7808-4640-9f29-d552bd76f673", "type": "concept", "title": "ANSI A117.1, ASCE 7, NFPA standards, ASTM standards, UL listings, and product approvals"}, {"id": "4376ba7a-739f-4b39-9c0f-830484df9e77", "type": "concept", "title": "Edition conflicts and adopted-reference tables"}, {"id": "d4ae012a-6031-4ce1-b22a-ffadadd84987", "type": "concept", "title": "Manufacturer data, tested assemblies, and delegated design"}], "description": "The gist is to know that codes incorporate many external technical standards."}, {"id": "137006d8-febe-43c2-954d-612edc24efcb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Code navigation skills", "children": [{"id": "e4b46585-1081-4725-9cff-ccb3b86f9e42", "type": "concept", "title": "Definitions, scoping, exceptions, tables, footnotes, cross-references, and commentary"}, {"id": "b5c1f425-f886-48e2-97e1-d32163cbd435", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading chapter structure before isolated sections"}, {"id": "af5abf32-3561-4bae-a084-e4425574a270", "type": "concept", "title": "Documenting code citations and assumptions"}], "description": "Students learn to move from question to enforceable answer."}]}, {"id": "4d6b7f57-e346-446d-a60e-17fb7de6d3b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Occupancy Classification, Use, Special Occupancies, and Mixed-Use Buildings", "description": "Students learn to classify building uses correctly because occupancy drives nearly every downstream code decision, from egress to fire protection to allowable area.", "children": [{"id": "0a3833aa-1e9d-4f71-ab2e-891e0805b44f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Occupancy groups", "children": [{"id": "5ee8c145-fa64-41b4-a76c-120e69621668", "type": "concept", "title": "Assembly, business, educational, factory, high-hazard, institutional, mercantile, residential, storage, and utility uses"}, {"id": "3463bc10-d973-489a-aad1-94f317eaa035", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessory spaces and incidental uses"}, {"id": "452d5d7f-624b-4c0d-85d2-c7cd1ca393cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Special occupancies and high-risk conditions"}], "description": "Students learn to classify uses because classification drives the rest of code analysis."}, {"id": "d80dcc60-80b5-43c8-aadd-3624d4caaee2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mixed occupancy", "children": [{"id": "33e5542e-c0d7-49a6-966c-476f55de629c", "type": "concept", "title": "Separated and nonseparated mixed occupancies"}, {"id": "f638c116-d07b-4862-a4b9-d2a16d755c52", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessory occupancies and aggregate accessory use"}, {"id": "648e9acc-425f-4a3f-83b7-6b7c7ff1ddf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Most restrictive provisions and fire separation requirements"}], "description": "The point is to handle real buildings with multiple uses."}, {"id": "39bcf6dd-7fff-4610-9ea4-39434d4dd51e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Programming implications", "children": [{"id": "0b8354e8-dcb6-43ce-841f-e230fcb33467", "type": "concept", "title": "Program stacking, adjacency, entry sequence, support spaces, and tenant divisions"}, {"id": "a354d400-36d1-47cb-924d-49feb79a87bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant characteristics and operational assumptions"}, {"id": "8eb52c15-51f2-454d-af2b-f26ffa8d2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Code strategy before schematic design locks in risk"}], "description": "Students connect code classification to early design choices."}]}, {"id": "80a7fac4-5c9a-45de-97f9-742e24016568", "type": "topic", "title": "Construction Types, Fire Resistance, Combustibility, Height, Area, and Building Separation", "description": "This topic connects building materials and assemblies to construction type, fire-resistance ratings, allowable height/area, frontage, sprinklers, and property-line constraints.", "children": [{"id": "21f3ced5-ee9d-4a7a-b659-c1afaa0b1173", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Construction type", "children": [{"id": "5ff7323e-8532-402f-a7c9-e480e9e630b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Types I, II, III, IV, and V"}, {"id": "76d669ba-1531-41ca-8880-62170ccaafc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Combustible and noncombustible construction"}, {"id": "db4f1722-fc9c-4586-93f8-d65ea431474a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass timber, heavy timber, concrete, steel, masonry, and wood framing"}], "description": "Students learn how structural systems and materials affect code allowances."}, {"id": "de4ca75b-8be7-411b-a622-b2c6a1b99d90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fire-resistance ratings", "children": [{"id": "46d2af16-9eca-45c7-968a-4ca63b0874e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural frame, bearing walls, floors, roofs, shafts, corridors, and occupancy separations"}, {"id": "f825a5fc-af8b-42e9-8afe-452213dbc3e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Tested assemblies, listings, penetrations, joints, and continuity"}, {"id": "915bbc9d-c5cf-4090-afd0-fbb1476f8b4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire walls, fire barriers, fire partitions, smoke barriers, and horizontal assemblies"}], "description": "The gist is to connect assemblies to rating requirements."}, {"id": "1bb118e8-5cc1-4b78-b2a4-91f463aee8bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Allowable building size", "children": [{"id": "40296c20-63b3-4ef8-9717-18a4b9074a64", "type": "concept", "title": "Allowable area, frontage increases, sprinkler increases, story limits, and basements"}, {"id": "9e509112-359b-45d6-a984-67f473e47328", "type": "concept", "title": "Property line, fire separation distance, exposure, and exterior wall openings"}, {"id": "83b6cc35-11d7-4ed9-bdf3-b36672745430", "type": "concept", "title": "Mixed-use and podium implications"}], "description": "Students practice height and area reasoning."}]}, {"id": "6de59046-ffc4-4c99-aa74-a3513728c1d4", "type": "topic", "title": "Fire and Life Safety Systems: Compartmentation, Fire Protection, Alarms, Smoke Control, and NFPA Coordination", "description": "Students study the building code's life-safety logic and how fire-resistance, detection, suppression, alarm, and smoke-control systems work together.", "children": [{"id": "c82de524-ebe9-47bd-a916-afe6042d47cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Passive fire protection", "children": [{"id": "fd7fa019-4d9e-476a-9c47-6047bf78841f", "type": "concept", "title": "Compartmentation, rated enclosures, opening protectives, shafts, atria, and horizontal exits"}, {"id": "718a7d8b-287d-4cad-9f0d-59cc03e94876", "type": "concept", "title": "Continuity, penetrations, dampers, and rated glazing"}, {"id": "21ff2f9a-44ee-4877-b2b9-2658cdf98c72", "type": "concept", "title": "Details that preserve ratings during construction"}], "description": "Students learn how building form and assemblies slow fire and smoke spread."}, {"id": "6c005119-ed14-40db-9c3d-af0e9c0e91d6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Active systems", "children": [{"id": "7484d67d-b848-4557-ba77-51962fa6c30a", "type": "concept", "title": "Automatic sprinklers, standpipes, fire alarms, detection, emergency voice/alarm communication, and smoke control"}, {"id": "b3532524-97c3-462f-b48b-1ad5cf8cc8da", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire department connections and fire command centers"}, {"id": "32f1f5fa-87f5-4c0c-a6a2-18a729ef5e2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with fire protection engineers and equipment rooms"}], "description": "The point is to coordinate code requirements with engineering systems."}, {"id": "726cff1c-5ab3-444d-9ace-f545126c979e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "NFPA and fire-code interface", "children": [{"id": "1eeb37b2-46b3-425b-9ca7-47564f0f0e19", "type": "concept", "title": "NFPA 101, NFPA 13, NFPA 72, NEC/NFPA 70, and referenced standards"}, {"id": "f3176131-5ba4-47f9-a5a8-dc4cfca409b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Operational fire-code requirements after occupancy"}, {"id": "f9da7f4d-e373-41a3-9ada-63c5e62d04d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with fire marshal review"}], "description": "Students understand where building code and fire code meet."}]}, {"id": "6307d6f9-6092-4b5d-a7cf-24d8f0c03dea", "type": "topic", "title": "Means of Egress: Occupant Load, Exit Access, Exits, Discharge, Travel Distance, Stairs, Doors, and Signage", "description": "This module teaches the egress path as a complete system that must be sized, arranged, protected, and documented for predictable evacuation.", "children": [{"id": "4cf8d568-0f45-4e6e-a78b-72af6e140254", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Occupant load and egress capacity", "children": [{"id": "44be493b-53b1-45ae-bd39-ea267be35de2", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant load factors, concentrated loads, cumulative loads, and mixed spaces"}, {"id": "046d3db5-ccc5-4666-969e-3103e38b4c06", "type": "concept", "title": "Door, corridor, stair, ramp, and exit width"}, {"id": "e85586b3-fc16-46fb-834f-1251b2211c7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Common path, dead end, and travel distance"}], "description": "Students learn to size egress from actual use and code factors."}, {"id": "e65104cd-2ced-47ca-bf69-f92611b52cd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Egress system components", "children": [{"id": "e4d79c0a-fc12-4b38-9db3-e6a293a40e82", "type": "concept", "title": "Exit access, exit, exit discharge, horizontal exits, areas of refuge, exit passageways, and public way"}, {"id": "91239392-6c0d-4c52-ab08-41ee0305f162", "type": "concept", "title": "Stairs, ramps, doors, gates, panic hardware, handrails, guards, and landings"}, {"id": "262fb864-1340-475e-a198-76e11ad1e166", "type": "concept", "title": "Exit signs, emergency lighting, tactile signage, and wayfinding"}], "description": "The gist is to follow the full path to a public way."}, {"id": "3ee969e8-3f4c-4540-9478-d17206183783", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Egress documentation", "children": [{"id": "2894f791-57da-4ae0-b711-e9b0d8687e5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-safety plans, egress diagrams, occupant-load tables, exit capacity tables, and code notes"}, {"id": "99a7a40a-99bf-4197-b5cc-f3e2422426f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple floors, assembly spaces, and accessibility interactions"}, {"id": "8d3ecf02-563e-4513-92a2-3bea07b84764", "type": "concept", "title": "Reviewer comments and revision tracking"}], "description": "Students turn calculations into reviewer-ready drawings."}]}, {"id": "94a149e2-c577-46a3-acdb-515e33625f9f", "type": "topic", "title": "Accessibility: ADA, IBC Chapter 11, ANSI A117.1, Universal Design, and Inclusive User Experience", "description": "Students learn the difference between civil-rights accessibility requirements, model-code accessibility provisions, technical criteria, and broader universal design goals.", "children": [{"id": "4b19914f-9382-4d08-bfcc-ef6cb9bca395", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legal and code frameworks", "children": [{"id": "562260ea-14d5-441e-ab14-22e25c2ca0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "ADA Title II and Title III design standards"}, {"id": "eb28888d-df92-4305-9cff-ef3273250f5f", "type": "concept", "title": "IBC Chapter 11 and ANSI A117.1 technical criteria"}, {"id": "a1afec3e-16f4-45b7-bb3e-df8bfcc538da", "type": "concept", "title": "State/local accessibility amendments and Fair Housing Act context"}], "description": "Students learn the difference between civil-rights law and model-code requirements."}, {"id": "c8ca8ddb-c839-463c-9455-92b5c17799c3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technical accessibility", "children": [{"id": "61de57cc-11c7-4681-afe6-945dca5c7f15", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessible routes, entrances, doors, ramps, elevators, toilet rooms, parking, signage, assembly seating, and dwelling units"}, {"id": "bae7e39a-40b1-4c7c-acd4-372832520a03", "type": "concept", "title": "Reach ranges, clear floor space, turning space, protruding objects, and operable parts"}, {"id": "124576d0-8c28-4f8d-8617-c10416891ac5", "type": "concept", "title": "Alterations, barrier removal, program access, and equivalent facilitation"}], "description": "The point is to convert inclusive intent into measurable design criteria."}, {"id": "20a1d6b8-996e-4c33-974a-eb176b87367a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Universal design", "children": [{"id": "ab4f1047-e367-4b20-867e-b26f1dfa763f", "type": "concept", "title": "Equitable use, flexibility, intuitive use, low physical effort, and tolerance for error"}, {"id": "1de38a43-02aa-4702-b10c-84e13bf0bfdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Neurodiversity, sensory access, aging users, and temporary disability"}, {"id": "614983a3-a49e-42a6-9dcc-f70236592c0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive post-occupancy feedback"}], "description": "Students move beyond minimum compliance."}]}, {"id": "8f057875-e958-4b56-b65f-40c4a791d22d", "type": "topic", "title": "Public Health, Plumbing Fixtures, Sanitation, Ventilation, Interior Environment, and Basic MEP Coordination", "description": "This topic introduces public-health code issues architects must coordinate early: fixture counts, toilet rooms, drinking fountains, ventilation, environmental quality, and systems interfaces.", "children": [{"id": "c8aa4d1d-70f6-4516-9649-b5b230ee243b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fixture counts and toilet rooms", "children": [{"id": "6a7f1146-ba93-4cab-a0ff-4cf403acd29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Required plumbing fixture calculations"}, {"id": "9c5a799a-bfec-486a-a481-353594bb1ef3", "type": "concept", "title": "Toilet rooms, drinking fountains, service sinks, bathing facilities, and family/assisted-use rooms"}, {"id": "1ed1d5f3-4eb0-477f-8679-1b58da5dab6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and gender-inclusive planning"}], "description": "Students learn how occupancy drives public-health requirements."}, {"id": "024fcfe3-5d26-43b8-a81c-e8fd85a9884e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environmental health basics", "children": [{"id": "327d472f-a9d6-4119-bef7-36c03172dab5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ventilation, exhaust, natural light, sanitation, moisture, hazardous materials, and pest control"}, {"id": "192ebb99-eb6a-462b-b124-bba9cb248287", "type": "concept", "title": "Toilet-room separation, food service, laboratories, and special uses"}, {"id": "d4ff35e9-af01-471a-9d5c-0433c1a4c9a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with mechanical and plumbing engineers"}], "description": "The gist is to understand how code protects indoor health."}, {"id": "c4bc1c27-09f7-41f1-845a-7e992bc1f153", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architectural coordination", "children": [{"id": "a3ecbb06-7744-404d-85b3-da387206e760", "type": "concept", "title": "Shafts, chases, equipment rooms, access panels, ceiling coordination, and roof equipment"}, {"id": "478039bb-8d77-4428-a0f1-674d240dd038", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire/smoke dampers, rated penetrations, and access clearances"}, {"id": "51cbcaf6-c0a1-4343-abf3-c6208fdec175", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflected ceiling plans and enlarged plans"}], "description": "Students learn to reserve space for compliant systems early."}]}, {"id": "ae5550b3-9420-4bff-944a-6400f85d70e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Energy Codes, Envelope Compliance, Mechanical Coordination, Commissioning, Climate Zones, and Sustainability", "description": "Students learn how energy codes shape envelope design, systems coordination, compliance paths, and climate-responsive design decisions.", "children": [{"id": "33306aeb-49db-4986-9c7c-8556d73c30e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Energy-code frameworks", "children": [{"id": "4d07ea2a-d7bf-4959-a670-56a566a150dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate zones, building type, residential/commercial scope, and adopted editions"}, {"id": "36925eed-6a6b-46b1-8786-5b6eef1ff264", "type": "concept", "title": "Prescriptive, trade-off, performance, and simulated performance paths"}, {"id": "efed1257-d21c-42a4-976b-74a4a8ff7959", "type": "concept", "title": "State energy-code portals and compliance tools"}], "description": "Students learn the role of IECC, ASHRAE 90.1, state energy codes, and local stretch codes."}, {"id": "eb670195-0f1d-4d0e-a958-1251d9fb7329", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Envelope compliance", "children": [{"id": "9f020219-98bb-4471-9eb7-cfc8cdd132ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Insulation, U-factor, SHGC, air leakage, thermal bridges, vestibules, and daylighting"}, {"id": "1ec5bc71-103a-4630-a29f-d54299a408d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Envelope details, testing, and commissioning"}, {"id": "f668cbbf-8f26-48f2-8f12-f2271ecce5bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with facade consultants and energy modelers"}], "description": "The point is to connect code to actual wall, roof, window, and air-barrier design."}, {"id": "ac63b2ee-35bc-4d65-b038-c3f492672221", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Systems and sustainability", "children": [{"id": "8d395bf2-1574-4597-b403-94171974d7db", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical efficiency, lighting controls, service water heating, commissioning, and renewable readiness"}, {"id": "1b87e1bf-d718-4e58-982c-ace09187afa6", "type": "concept", "title": "LEED, Passive House, resilience, and carbon goals as overlays"}, {"id": "fde43f75-c5eb-4d7e-a1b0-ff9fa7427ed0", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy documentation and permit submissions"}], "description": "Students see where code minimums meet high-performance design."}]}, {"id": "bdd46ae8-a51f-471b-a669-b30426db4a53", "type": "topic", "title": "Zoning, Land Use, Site Controls, Overlays, Environmental Constraints, and Resilience", "description": "This module distinguishes building codes from zoning and teaches students to read site entitlements, setbacks, FAR, parking, overlays, flood zones, wildfire exposure, and local constraints.", "children": [{"id": "a9d6e395-2541-4b4f-9690-f4c0fbedc1f8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Zoning basics", "children": [{"id": "1c44f825-9ae5-4033-a312-d72533669d86", "type": "concept", "title": "Use, district, setbacks, height, FAR, lot coverage, density, parking, loading, and signage"}, {"id": "e19ffbca-6164-42b5-a14d-5d9d38531c7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Variances, special permits, conditional uses, and design review"}, {"id": "1b96f7a4-e488-4ccf-8507-56ed435aaba6", "type": "concept", "title": "Entitlement risk and schedule impact"}], "description": "Students distinguish land-use approvals from building-code approvals."}, {"id": "262b8f01-2227-499a-96a2-2305521abb8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Site and environmental constraints", "children": [{"id": "87a91404-9559-432b-be57-3e6268c56f2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Flood zones, coastal zones, wildfire interface, seismic zones, wetlands, stormwater, easements, and rights-of-way"}, {"id": "9c0c4a5c-5b91-4791-8f47-43a2af6d2bb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Historic districts, overlay zones, and neighborhood review"}, {"id": "3f0206dc-c188-447f-8973-29fcdec0ef76", "type": "concept", "title": "Utility, access, emergency vehicle, and fire apparatus constraints"}], "description": "The gist is to identify external controls before design commitments harden."}, {"id": "462cd9e6-e3a2-4732-9bd6-079652974986", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resilience and local regulation", "children": [{"id": "83ade896-667a-4ae9-b022-37ba29a86efe", "type": "concept", "title": "Floodproofing, freeboard, defensible space, heat, storm shelter, and backup power"}, {"id": "3f352d1b-687e-4005-a768-7bfd11524421", "type": "concept", "title": "Local sustainability ordinances and benchmarking"}, {"id": "d4d8fc4e-44fb-4ba8-a98a-fb4bcf607481", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with civil, landscape, and environmental consultants"}], "description": "Students learn how local regulation responds to climate and hazard conditions."}]}, {"id": "2bb7da85-8e53-4cdf-99b0-746aaa3e140e", "type": "topic", "title": "Existing Buildings, Alterations, Adaptive Reuse, Historic Preservation, Change of Occupancy, and IEBC Strategy", "description": "Students learn how existing-building work differs from new construction and how code strategy can enable safe, feasible reuse without erasing building history.", "children": [{"id": "a28a724e-c7b7-4d3d-bf83-a6be3f728b95", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Existing-building code paths", "children": [{"id": "a05d2d0b-49a0-436d-b633-593c18e28f0d", "type": "concept", "title": "IEBC work area, prescriptive, performance, and compliance methods"}, {"id": "fe3d1431-3fa2-406d-8e5c-283f01bbf73d", "type": "concept", "title": "Repairs, alterations, additions, relocation, and change of occupancy"}, {"id": "61e73890-373f-445e-8e24-7551c1ad075f", "type": "concept", "title": "Trigger points and scoping decisions"}], "description": "Students learn that renovation is not simply new-construction code applied backwards."}, {"id": "b587a0ef-81e0-4f11-b2d8-450a175508ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adaptive reuse strategy", "children": [{"id": "6c2edbad-d1e5-4b38-bee1-135fa8d67e0b", "type": "concept", "title": "Existing conditions surveys, code deficiency matrices, structural and fire/life-safety upgrades"}, {"id": "c85352e7-783b-4a17-9544-1d21bfe9dc8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Egress, accessibility, hazardous materials, and systems replacement"}, {"id": "aef859c9-5645-4434-b574-2fb42f941a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Phasing, tenant occupation, and constructability"}], "description": "The point is to make reuse safe and feasible."}, {"id": "9f30f8b9-e3b2-4c57-a40d-1251d66ad6b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historic preservation", "children": [{"id": "399a227e-9c8d-48d1-b5d2-c15964834301", "type": "concept", "title": "Historic building provisions, preservation standards, character-defining features, and alternative compliance"}, {"id": "90a64331-4ec5-4362-b081-2c15cbcac22c", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and life-safety upgrades in historic fabric"}, {"id": "8673b44d-42ee-45d3-b97a-202cf9826261", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with preservation boards and AHJs"}], "description": "Students balance public safety with cultural value."}]}, {"id": "401e8710-2ef1-454a-a31d-0ec25b34d66f", "type": "topic", "title": "Code Analysis Drawings, Life-Safety Plans, Diagrams, Schedules, and Compliance Narratives", "description": "This topic turns code interpretation into architectural documentation: plans, diagrams, tables, narratives, and reviewer-ready evidence.", "children": [{"id": "23303653-a06c-4519-850c-a8d0554caa94", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Code sheets", "children": [{"id": "132e1104-ec25-4e5b-90f2-b6463f42f06d", "type": "concept", "title": "Applicable codes, edition, jurisdiction, occupancy, construction type, area, height, sprinklers, and special inspections"}, {"id": "f215755a-d82c-463f-b195-04cd2b7f7520", "type": "concept", "title": "Code summary sheets and project data"}, {"id": "7ae9acb9-9151-42b6-97af-6668909271f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions, exceptions, and alternates"}], "description": "Students learn what reviewers need to see quickly."}, {"id": "a2af39c2-b82e-47f6-ba97-ad78628863b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Life-safety drawings", "children": [{"id": "2f8286c1-07cb-4e4e-b2cf-589021f6f893", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant-load plans, egress paths, exit widths, travel distances, rated walls, smoke barriers, and fire extinguishers"}, {"id": "e05660b9-92b2-4f02-8266-c048ce0bc4a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility diagrams and plumbing fixture counts"}, {"id": "e0f2ed1c-9fbe-451e-ac95-106aa9605a16", "type": "concept", "title": "Graphic hierarchy and reviewer legibility"}], "description": "The gist is to make compliance visible."}, {"id": "6cbc0b6c-a5d9-4c5a-bdf5-570b6d01fea5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narratives and matrices", "children": [{"id": "9de7abce-fa07-411a-a7bf-0413e8f36af6", "type": "concept", "title": "Compliance narratives, code modification requests, alternate means and methods, and variance support"}, {"id": "68081cdd-7ca9-41da-bb46-feb5c7f54ca0", "type": "concept", "title": "Comment response logs and revision clouds"}, {"id": "384974ef-9525-4877-a607-b94475e61ddf", "type": "concept", "title": "Clear citation and evidence standards"}], "description": "Students practice written code reasoning."}]}, {"id": "8af55417-2179-48af-95f7-ac9e34511857", "type": "topic", "title": "Plan Review, Permitting, Inspections, Deferred Submittals, Special Inspections, and AHJ Communication", "description": "Students learn the regulatory workflow from pre-application research through permit submission, comments, revisions, inspection, and closeout.", "children": [{"id": "355a1c2b-8303-429e-b1e9-d9a79d51b40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Permit workflow", "children": [{"id": "1fb8baf0-1a53-4dc7-addc-d439d661a1ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-submittal meetings, permit applications, drawing completeness, fees, and intake checks"}, {"id": "d63bf8a6-3c52-4c14-bf3b-9929fe62b454", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan review cycles, comments, resubmittals, approvals, and permits"}, {"id": "ca352e39-279e-46a7-84ef-cd061e9ea941", "type": "concept", "title": "Fast-track, phased, foundation-only, and tenant permits"}], "description": "Students learn how projects move through review."}, {"id": "6968f9df-657e-481b-a1fd-11ec05e012f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Construction-phase compliance", "children": [{"id": "3ecf3028-e7cd-42a2-a983-60138398ecc7", "type": "concept", "title": "Inspections, special inspections, deferred submittals, shop drawings, substitutions, and field changes"}, {"id": "b056a46b-7488-4ec8-965d-79016c64d4de", "type": "concept", "title": "Requests for information, ASIs, CCDs, and change orders"}, {"id": "99c608f8-2583-47aa-9040-580271ed04b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Certificate of occupancy and closeout"}], "description": "The point is to keep code decisions alive after permit approval."}, {"id": "2c25f33e-6a9a-4c3d-adcc-0bf72ea2c18a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AHJ communication", "children": [{"id": "e5061e7e-1f6d-4ca9-bc28-c456aa3b65b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Meeting agendas, code questions, written interpretations, and decision logs"}, {"id": "5320e545-a34a-4049-a760-9834fe0b2d51", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectful negotiation and documentation"}, {"id": "40496df5-9f04-4922-abc3-3ebae06de8e6", "type": "concept", "title": "When to escalate to consultants, attorneys, or senior leadership"}], "description": "Students learn professional communication with reviewers."}]}, {"id": "3690c5c0-b874-417a-a513-b6bb72a3b3c7", "type": "topic", "title": "Risk, Ethics, Professional Responsibility, Equity, and Code Judgment", "description": "This module addresses the architect's responsibility to protect public welfare while navigating ambiguity, minimum requirements, professional liability, cost pressure, and equitable access.", "children": [{"id": "70232667-eeb9-4078-8afe-31702554a1df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public welfare duty", "children": [{"id": "d7f1f73b-d139-4c3b-a9f0-f74713140bd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Health, safety, welfare, licensure, standard of care, and responsible control"}, {"id": "ac297f3b-db79-46d1-bf1c-b5675d448183", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum code versus foreseeable risk"}, {"id": "e1b1890d-f977-4375-8fe9-d707b2356c8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation and decision accountability"}], "description": "Students connect codes to professional ethics."}, {"id": "51b7b0a4-9eed-46bc-985b-141c499c2c21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ambiguity and judgment", "children": [{"id": "062f9673-e7c4-4753-b1b5-f4718b3f6c02", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflicting provisions, unclear definitions, alternative interpretations, and local practices"}, {"id": "683148c4-7312-400f-b1c9-e52695089bc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Appeals, equivalencies, and performance-based approaches"}, {"id": "311dd005-2e1a-4433-aa0e-2cb9cf1d538a", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk registers and peer review"}], "description": "The gist is to manage uncertain code situations without pretending they are simple."}, {"id": "ceb9279d-8088-4d74-a2fc-04baa1caa604", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equity and access", "children": [{"id": "6dd609b0-fcf7-42ef-8615-4511162a3f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility, affordability, housing supply, climate resilience, and public safety tradeoffs"}, {"id": "7592248e-f728-4249-987b-d09524fe0225", "type": "concept", "title": "Unintended consequences of regulation"}, {"id": "7d98a27d-d87e-4595-bd89-da45b6849e09", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical client advising and user-centered code strategy"}], "description": "Students learn that compliance choices affect real users."}]}, {"id": "d915b53d-af0a-47ac-aeab-0aa7ae0c15ee", "type": "topic", "title": "Applied Code Integration Capstone: Code-Compliant Design, Redlines, Review Response, and Final Package", "description": "Students synthesize the course by applying code analysis to a real or studio project and producing a professional review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.", "children": [{"id": "a4c9f1f3-45c5-4fed-be20-ea9cc01b5f90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project selection and baseline analysis", "children": [{"id": "b78f4cd5-7f39-4a6c-a1e7-e15651c561b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Jurisdiction, adopted codes, occupancy, construction type, site constraints, and project scope"}, {"id": "d96bcbfd-4157-4f3a-b680-b87f79e3f2b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Existing or new building assumptions"}, {"id": "f4dd56ba-5585-4fc7-ba74-22446885bbbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Initial code-risk memo"}], "description": "Students define a realistic building or studio project for applied code work."}, {"id": "0837bc99-80c8-42f3-a71c-fcf901aedcdc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Design integration", "children": [{"id": "d1ecc8ca-6817-4fcd-81fc-9f1338647d80", "type": "concept", "title": "Egress layout, accessibility route, rated assemblies, fixture counts, energy approach, and zoning constraints"}, {"id": "2b91f387-16b6-4a3b-a577-86fa49273d69", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with structure, envelope, MEP, and fire protection"}, {"id": "37eb6a3f-d896-4a29-8c25-c0b35c6d05f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Redlines and design alternatives"}], "description": "The point is to revise design from code evidence rather than bolt compliance on at the end."}, {"id": "559797c5-6a35-4ad3-ad5e-0a428d956cbc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final package", "children": [{"id": "70874ad1-32bb-4588-a667-621b9d37319f", "type": "concept", "title": "Code summary, life-safety plan, accessibility audit, egress calculations, zoning matrix, and permit narrative"}, {"id": "3b4334f2-4c39-4dc9-94a5-c92a9c7b417d", "type": "concept", "title": "Reviewer comment response simulation"}, {"id": "834e8082-621f-43d7-b6bd-f9c594a2202a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection on judgment, ethics, and lessons learned"}], "description": "Students produce professional deliverables."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$bcdrjson$::jsonb,
  true,
  false,
  'course',
  'public'
where not exists (
  select 1 from public.learning_paths where slug = 'building-codes-and-regulations'
);

update public.learning_paths
set
  title = 'Building Codes and Regulations',
  goal = 'Building Codes and Regulations',
  summary = 'Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and the authority having jurisdiction. The course covers I-Code navigation, occupancy and mixed use, construction type and allowable area, fire and life safety, means of egress, accessibility and universal design, public-health and MEP coordination, energy codes, zoning and site constraints, existing buildings, code-analysis documentation, permitting, and professional responsibility. A capstone applies code analysis to a design and produces a review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.',
  data = $bcdrjson${"slug": "building-codes-and-regulations", "title": "Building Codes and Regulations", "description": "Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and the authority having jurisdiction. The course covers I-Code navigation, occupancy and mixed use, construction type and allowable area, fire and life safety, means of egress, accessibility and universal design, public-health and MEP coordination, energy codes, zoning and site constraints, existing buildings, code-analysis documentation, permitting, and professional responsibility. A capstone applies code analysis to a design and produces a review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.", "topics": [{"id": "068d46b5-c594-4aba-8a7f-91dd593e4c3d", "type": "topic", "title": "U.S. Building Regulation Ecosystem: Public Safety, Model Codes, Adoption, AHJs, and Jurisdiction", "description": "Students learn how American building regulation actually works: model codes become enforceable only through state or local adoption, amendments, plan review, inspections, and authority having jurisdiction.", "children": [{"id": "ce71d5e6-759d-4327-aebe-2d40c3db1d7e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Purpose and limits of codes", "children": [{"id": "fe12ede4-7a80-45aa-9c25-f7a23fb1cac2", "type": "concept", "title": "Life safety, health, accessibility, energy, resilience, and public welfare"}, {"id": "e31e2b1e-fbda-4d80-9fcd-14ea65f10866", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum compliance versus best practice"}, {"id": "106757df-7c0c-46ba-bd96-62e19980761a", "type": "concept", "title": "Code intent, prescriptive paths, performance paths, and equivalencies"}], "description": "Students learn that codes set minimum public-safety requirements, not the full measure of good design."}, {"id": "02f1c441-31a2-47cb-801f-223ebf715841", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Jurisdictional adoption", "children": [{"id": "2d905d28-b469-4c5c-8753-5417099d6e47", "type": "concept", "title": "State and municipal adoption cycles"}, {"id": "ddd57cba-cc0a-4499-b27e-883135edef42", "type": "concept", "title": "Local amendments, supplements, and administrative provisions"}, {"id": "9b636562-56f5-4a94-8c3e-94f1a8855f61", "type": "concept", "title": "Authority having jurisdiction, building official, fire marshal, zoning staff, and special reviewers"}], "description": "The point is to understand why the applicable code is local even when the model code is national."}, {"id": "ee5a6d36-0844-4a85-889c-b49fd866f971", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Regulatory workflow", "children": [{"id": "9a08d723-2ec7-4e71-8e97-3d38ae9ae9df", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-application research, permit triggers, plan review, inspections, certificates of occupancy"}, {"id": "1cabced9-1c8e-4afd-ab82-cb3390529d69", "type": "concept", "title": "New construction, alterations, additions, changes of use, and tenant improvements"}, {"id": "29876e87-3de4-4125-a34c-b9205be449cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation, communication, and recordkeeping"}], "description": "Students see how code research becomes approvals."}]}, {"id": "de07ea01-3cd3-4e8e-ac73-f5e5abc7a60d", "type": "topic", "title": "International Codes Framework: IBC, IRC, IEBC, IFC, IECC, Referenced Standards, and Code Navigation", "description": "This module teaches students to navigate the I-Code family and understand how building, residential, existing building, fire, energy, mechanical, plumbing, structural, and referenced standards interact.", "children": [{"id": "2397f29c-5c95-4031-bcac-6488bf82e7e9", "type": "subtopic", "title": "I-Code family", "children": [{"id": "a0a3818a-0850-4bd5-b652-6d92ee485e10", "type": "concept", "title": "IBC, IRC, IEBC, IFC, IECC, IMC, IPC, IFGC, IgCC, and zoning references"}, {"id": "992bb78d-26fd-4b71-92d9-8d0c320f1d11", "type": "concept", "title": "Commercial versus residential scope"}, {"id": "6a1c642b-f0ba-433d-96d4-96ac73fd857e", "type": "concept", "title": "Building, fire, energy, mechanical, plumbing, and existing-building interfaces"}], "description": "Students learn which model code governs which kind of problem."}, {"id": "1e377454-4d59-4065-89da-831ceddd7c8e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Referenced standards", "children": [{"id": "f1a40993-7808-4640-9f29-d552bd76f673", "type": "concept", "title": "ANSI A117.1, ASCE 7, NFPA standards, ASTM standards, UL listings, and product approvals"}, {"id": "4376ba7a-739f-4b39-9c0f-830484df9e77", "type": "concept", "title": "Edition conflicts and adopted-reference tables"}, {"id": "d4ae012a-6031-4ce1-b22a-ffadadd84987", "type": "concept", "title": "Manufacturer data, tested assemblies, and delegated design"}], "description": "The gist is to know that codes incorporate many external technical standards."}, {"id": "137006d8-febe-43c2-954d-612edc24efcb", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Code navigation skills", "children": [{"id": "e4b46585-1081-4725-9cff-ccb3b86f9e42", "type": "concept", "title": "Definitions, scoping, exceptions, tables, footnotes, cross-references, and commentary"}, {"id": "b5c1f425-f886-48e2-97e1-d32163cbd435", "type": "concept", "title": "Reading chapter structure before isolated sections"}, {"id": "af5abf32-3561-4bae-a084-e4425574a270", "type": "concept", "title": "Documenting code citations and assumptions"}], "description": "Students learn to move from question to enforceable answer."}]}, {"id": "4d6b7f57-e346-446d-a60e-17fb7de6d3b6", "type": "topic", "title": "Occupancy Classification, Use, Special Occupancies, and Mixed-Use Buildings", "description": "Students learn to classify building uses correctly because occupancy drives nearly every downstream code decision, from egress to fire protection to allowable area.", "children": [{"id": "0a3833aa-1e9d-4f71-ab2e-891e0805b44f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Occupancy groups", "children": [{"id": "5ee8c145-fa64-41b4-a76c-120e69621668", "type": "concept", "title": "Assembly, business, educational, factory, high-hazard, institutional, mercantile, residential, storage, and utility uses"}, {"id": "3463bc10-d973-489a-aad1-94f317eaa035", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessory spaces and incidental uses"}, {"id": "452d5d7f-624b-4c0d-85d2-c7cd1ca393cb", "type": "concept", "title": "Special occupancies and high-risk conditions"}], "description": "Students learn to classify uses because classification drives the rest of code analysis."}, {"id": "d80dcc60-80b5-43c8-aadd-3624d4caaee2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Mixed occupancy", "children": [{"id": "33e5542e-c0d7-49a6-966c-476f55de629c", "type": "concept", "title": "Separated and nonseparated mixed occupancies"}, {"id": "f638c116-d07b-4862-a4b9-d2a16d755c52", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessory occupancies and aggregate accessory use"}, {"id": "648e9acc-425f-4a3f-83b7-6b7c7ff1ddf0", "type": "concept", "title": "Most restrictive provisions and fire separation requirements"}], "description": "The point is to handle real buildings with multiple uses."}, {"id": "39bcf6dd-7fff-4610-9ea4-39434d4dd51e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Programming implications", "children": [{"id": "0b8354e8-dcb6-43ce-841f-e230fcb33467", "type": "concept", "title": "Program stacking, adjacency, entry sequence, support spaces, and tenant divisions"}, {"id": "a354d400-36d1-47cb-924d-49feb79a87bc", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant characteristics and operational assumptions"}, {"id": "8eb52c15-51f2-454d-af2b-f26ffa8d2760", "type": "concept", "title": "Code strategy before schematic design locks in risk"}], "description": "Students connect code classification to early design choices."}]}, {"id": "80a7fac4-5c9a-45de-97f9-742e24016568", "type": "topic", "title": "Construction Types, Fire Resistance, Combustibility, Height, Area, and Building Separation", "description": "This topic connects building materials and assemblies to construction type, fire-resistance ratings, allowable height/area, frontage, sprinklers, and property-line constraints.", "children": [{"id": "21f3ced5-ee9d-4a7a-b659-c1afaa0b1173", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Construction type", "children": [{"id": "5ff7323e-8532-402f-a7c9-e480e9e630b0", "type": "concept", "title": "Types I, II, III, IV, and V"}, {"id": "76d669ba-1531-41ca-8880-62170ccaafc9", "type": "concept", "title": "Combustible and noncombustible construction"}, {"id": "db4f1722-fc9c-4586-93f8-d65ea431474a", "type": "concept", "title": "Mass timber, heavy timber, concrete, steel, masonry, and wood framing"}], "description": "Students learn how structural systems and materials affect code allowances."}, {"id": "de4ca75b-8be7-411b-a622-b2c6a1b99d90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fire-resistance ratings", "children": [{"id": "46d2af16-9eca-45c7-968a-4ca63b0874e4", "type": "concept", "title": "Structural frame, bearing walls, floors, roofs, shafts, corridors, and occupancy separations"}, {"id": "f825a5fc-af8b-42e9-8afe-452213dbc3e5", "type": "concept", "title": "Tested assemblies, listings, penetrations, joints, and continuity"}, {"id": "915bbc9d-c5cf-4090-afd0-fbb1476f8b4c", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire walls, fire barriers, fire partitions, smoke barriers, and horizontal assemblies"}], "description": "The gist is to connect assemblies to rating requirements."}, {"id": "1bb118e8-5cc1-4b78-b2a4-91f463aee8bc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Allowable building size", "children": [{"id": "40296c20-63b3-4ef8-9717-18a4b9074a64", "type": "concept", "title": "Allowable area, frontage increases, sprinkler increases, story limits, and basements"}, {"id": "9e509112-359b-45d6-a984-67f473e47328", "type": "concept", "title": "Property line, fire separation distance, exposure, and exterior wall openings"}, {"id": "83b6cc35-11d7-4ed9-bdf3-b36672745430", "type": "concept", "title": "Mixed-use and podium implications"}], "description": "Students practice height and area reasoning."}]}, {"id": "6de59046-ffc4-4c99-aa74-a3513728c1d4", "type": "topic", "title": "Fire and Life Safety Systems: Compartmentation, Fire Protection, Alarms, Smoke Control, and NFPA Coordination", "description": "Students study the building code's life-safety logic and how fire-resistance, detection, suppression, alarm, and smoke-control systems work together.", "children": [{"id": "c82de524-ebe9-47bd-a916-afe6042d47cc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Passive fire protection", "children": [{"id": "fd7fa019-4d9e-476a-9c47-6047bf78841f", "type": "concept", "title": "Compartmentation, rated enclosures, opening protectives, shafts, atria, and horizontal exits"}, {"id": "718a7d8b-287d-4cad-9f0d-59cc03e94876", "type": "concept", "title": "Continuity, penetrations, dampers, and rated glazing"}, {"id": "21ff2f9a-44ee-4877-b2b9-2658cdf98c72", "type": "concept", "title": "Details that preserve ratings during construction"}], "description": "Students learn how building form and assemblies slow fire and smoke spread."}, {"id": "6c005119-ed14-40db-9c3d-af0e9c0e91d6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Active systems", "children": [{"id": "7484d67d-b848-4557-ba77-51962fa6c30a", "type": "concept", "title": "Automatic sprinklers, standpipes, fire alarms, detection, emergency voice/alarm communication, and smoke control"}, {"id": "b3532524-97c3-462f-b48b-1ad5cf8cc8da", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire department connections and fire command centers"}, {"id": "32f1f5fa-87f5-4c0c-a6a2-18a729ef5e2b", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with fire protection engineers and equipment rooms"}], "description": "The point is to coordinate code requirements with engineering systems."}, {"id": "726cff1c-5ab3-444d-9ace-f545126c979e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "NFPA and fire-code interface", "children": [{"id": "1eeb37b2-46b3-425b-9ca7-47564f0f0e19", "type": "concept", "title": "NFPA 101, NFPA 13, NFPA 72, NEC/NFPA 70, and referenced standards"}, {"id": "f3176131-5ba4-47f9-a5a8-dc4cfca409b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Operational fire-code requirements after occupancy"}, {"id": "f9da7f4d-e373-41a3-9ada-63c5e62d04d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with fire marshal review"}], "description": "Students understand where building code and fire code meet."}]}, {"id": "6307d6f9-6092-4b5d-a7cf-24d8f0c03dea", "type": "topic", "title": "Means of Egress: Occupant Load, Exit Access, Exits, Discharge, Travel Distance, Stairs, Doors, and Signage", "description": "This module teaches the egress path as a complete system that must be sized, arranged, protected, and documented for predictable evacuation.", "children": [{"id": "4cf8d568-0f45-4e6e-a78b-72af6e140254", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Occupant load and egress capacity", "children": [{"id": "44be493b-53b1-45ae-bd39-ea267be35de2", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant load factors, concentrated loads, cumulative loads, and mixed spaces"}, {"id": "046d3db5-ccc5-4666-969e-3103e38b4c06", "type": "concept", "title": "Door, corridor, stair, ramp, and exit width"}, {"id": "e85586b3-fc16-46fb-834f-1251b2211c7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Common path, dead end, and travel distance"}], "description": "Students learn to size egress from actual use and code factors."}, {"id": "e65104cd-2ced-47ca-bf69-f92611b52cd6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Egress system components", "children": [{"id": "e4d79c0a-fc12-4b38-9db3-e6a293a40e82", "type": "concept", "title": "Exit access, exit, exit discharge, horizontal exits, areas of refuge, exit passageways, and public way"}, {"id": "91239392-6c0d-4c52-ab08-41ee0305f162", "type": "concept", "title": "Stairs, ramps, doors, gates, panic hardware, handrails, guards, and landings"}, {"id": "262fb864-1340-475e-a198-76e11ad1e166", "type": "concept", "title": "Exit signs, emergency lighting, tactile signage, and wayfinding"}], "description": "The gist is to follow the full path to a public way."}, {"id": "3ee969e8-3f4c-4540-9478-d17206183783", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Egress documentation", "children": [{"id": "2894f791-57da-4ae0-b711-e9b0d8687e5e", "type": "concept", "title": "Life-safety plans, egress diagrams, occupant-load tables, exit capacity tables, and code notes"}, {"id": "99a7a40a-99bf-4197-b5cc-f3e2422426f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Multiple floors, assembly spaces, and accessibility interactions"}, {"id": "8d3ecf02-563e-4513-92a2-3bea07b84764", "type": "concept", "title": "Reviewer comments and revision tracking"}], "description": "Students turn calculations into reviewer-ready drawings."}]}, {"id": "94a149e2-c577-46a3-acdb-515e33625f9f", "type": "topic", "title": "Accessibility: ADA, IBC Chapter 11, ANSI A117.1, Universal Design, and Inclusive User Experience", "description": "Students learn the difference between civil-rights accessibility requirements, model-code accessibility provisions, technical criteria, and broader universal design goals.", "children": [{"id": "4b19914f-9382-4d08-bfcc-ef6cb9bca395", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Legal and code frameworks", "children": [{"id": "562260ea-14d5-441e-ab14-22e25c2ca0d3", "type": "concept", "title": "ADA Title II and Title III design standards"}, {"id": "eb28888d-df92-4305-9cff-ef3273250f5f", "type": "concept", "title": "IBC Chapter 11 and ANSI A117.1 technical criteria"}, {"id": "a1afec3e-16f4-45b7-bb3e-df8bfcc538da", "type": "concept", "title": "State/local accessibility amendments and Fair Housing Act context"}], "description": "Students learn the difference between civil-rights law and model-code requirements."}, {"id": "c8ca8ddb-c839-463c-9455-92b5c17799c3", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Technical accessibility", "children": [{"id": "61de57cc-11c7-4681-afe6-945dca5c7f15", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessible routes, entrances, doors, ramps, elevators, toilet rooms, parking, signage, assembly seating, and dwelling units"}, {"id": "bae7e39a-40b1-4c7c-acd4-372832520a03", "type": "concept", "title": "Reach ranges, clear floor space, turning space, protruding objects, and operable parts"}, {"id": "124576d0-8c28-4f8d-8617-c10416891ac5", "type": "concept", "title": "Alterations, barrier removal, program access, and equivalent facilitation"}], "description": "The point is to convert inclusive intent into measurable design criteria."}, {"id": "20a1d6b8-996e-4c33-974a-eb176b87367a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Universal design", "children": [{"id": "ab4f1047-e367-4b20-867e-b26f1dfa763f", "type": "concept", "title": "Equitable use, flexibility, intuitive use, low physical effort, and tolerance for error"}, {"id": "1de38a43-02aa-4702-b10c-84e13bf0bfdd", "type": "concept", "title": "Neurodiversity, sensory access, aging users, and temporary disability"}, {"id": "614983a3-a49e-42a6-9dcc-f70236592c0e", "type": "concept", "title": "Inclusive post-occupancy feedback"}], "description": "Students move beyond minimum compliance."}]}, {"id": "8f057875-e958-4b56-b65f-40c4a791d22d", "type": "topic", "title": "Public Health, Plumbing Fixtures, Sanitation, Ventilation, Interior Environment, and Basic MEP Coordination", "description": "This topic introduces public-health code issues architects must coordinate early: fixture counts, toilet rooms, drinking fountains, ventilation, environmental quality, and systems interfaces.", "children": [{"id": "c8aa4d1d-70f6-4516-9649-b5b230ee243b", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Fixture counts and toilet rooms", "children": [{"id": "6a7f1146-ba93-4cab-a0ff-4cf403acd29d", "type": "concept", "title": "Required plumbing fixture calculations"}, {"id": "9c5a799a-bfec-486a-a481-353594bb1ef3", "type": "concept", "title": "Toilet rooms, drinking fountains, service sinks, bathing facilities, and family/assisted-use rooms"}, {"id": "1ed1d5f3-4eb0-477f-8679-1b58da5dab6d", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and gender-inclusive planning"}], "description": "Students learn how occupancy drives public-health requirements."}, {"id": "024fcfe3-5d26-43b8-a81c-e8fd85a9884e", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Environmental health basics", "children": [{"id": "327d472f-a9d6-4119-bef7-36c03172dab5", "type": "concept", "title": "Ventilation, exhaust, natural light, sanitation, moisture, hazardous materials, and pest control"}, {"id": "192ebb99-eb6a-462b-b124-bba9cb248287", "type": "concept", "title": "Toilet-room separation, food service, laboratories, and special uses"}, {"id": "d4ff35e9-af01-471a-9d5c-0433c1a4c9a2", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with mechanical and plumbing engineers"}], "description": "The gist is to understand how code protects indoor health."}, {"id": "c4bc1c27-09f7-41f1-845a-7e992bc1f153", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Architectural coordination", "children": [{"id": "a3ecbb06-7744-404d-85b3-da387206e760", "type": "concept", "title": "Shafts, chases, equipment rooms, access panels, ceiling coordination, and roof equipment"}, {"id": "478039bb-8d77-4428-a0f1-674d240dd038", "type": "concept", "title": "Fire/smoke dampers, rated penetrations, and access clearances"}, {"id": "51cbcaf6-c0a1-4343-abf3-c6208fdec175", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflected ceiling plans and enlarged plans"}], "description": "Students learn to reserve space for compliant systems early."}]}, {"id": "ae5550b3-9420-4bff-944a-6400f85d70e8", "type": "topic", "title": "Energy Codes, Envelope Compliance, Mechanical Coordination, Commissioning, Climate Zones, and Sustainability", "description": "Students learn how energy codes shape envelope design, systems coordination, compliance paths, and climate-responsive design decisions.", "children": [{"id": "33306aeb-49db-4986-9c7c-8556d73c30e0", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Energy-code frameworks", "children": [{"id": "4d07ea2a-d7bf-4959-a670-56a566a150dd", "type": "concept", "title": "Climate zones, building type, residential/commercial scope, and adopted editions"}, {"id": "36925eed-6a6b-46b1-8786-5b6eef1ff264", "type": "concept", "title": "Prescriptive, trade-off, performance, and simulated performance paths"}, {"id": "efed1257-d21c-42a4-976b-74a4a8ff7959", "type": "concept", "title": "State energy-code portals and compliance tools"}], "description": "Students learn the role of IECC, ASHRAE 90.1, state energy codes, and local stretch codes."}, {"id": "eb670195-0f1d-4d0e-a958-1251d9fb7329", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Envelope compliance", "children": [{"id": "9f020219-98bb-4471-9eb7-cfc8cdd132ac", "type": "concept", "title": "Insulation, U-factor, SHGC, air leakage, thermal bridges, vestibules, and daylighting"}, {"id": "1ec5bc71-103a-4630-a29f-d54299a408d6", "type": "concept", "title": "Envelope details, testing, and commissioning"}, {"id": "f668cbbf-8f26-48f2-8f12-f2271ecce5bb", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with facade consultants and energy modelers"}], "description": "The point is to connect code to actual wall, roof, window, and air-barrier design."}, {"id": "ac63b2ee-35bc-4d65-b038-c3f492672221", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Systems and sustainability", "children": [{"id": "8d395bf2-1574-4597-b403-94171974d7db", "type": "concept", "title": "Mechanical efficiency, lighting controls, service water heating, commissioning, and renewable readiness"}, {"id": "1b87e1bf-d718-4e58-982c-ace09187afa6", "type": "concept", "title": "LEED, Passive House, resilience, and carbon goals as overlays"}, {"id": "fde43f75-c5eb-4d7e-a1b0-ff9fa7427ed0", "type": "concept", "title": "Energy documentation and permit submissions"}], "description": "Students see where code minimums meet high-performance design."}]}, {"id": "bdd46ae8-a51f-471b-a669-b30426db4a53", "type": "topic", "title": "Zoning, Land Use, Site Controls, Overlays, Environmental Constraints, and Resilience", "description": "This module distinguishes building codes from zoning and teaches students to read site entitlements, setbacks, FAR, parking, overlays, flood zones, wildfire exposure, and local constraints.", "children": [{"id": "a9d6e395-2541-4b4f-9690-f4c0fbedc1f8", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Zoning basics", "children": [{"id": "1c44f825-9ae5-4033-a312-d72533669d86", "type": "concept", "title": "Use, district, setbacks, height, FAR, lot coverage, density, parking, loading, and signage"}, {"id": "e19ffbca-6164-42b5-a14d-5d9d38531c7f", "type": "concept", "title": "Variances, special permits, conditional uses, and design review"}, {"id": "1b96f7a4-e488-4ccf-8507-56ed435aaba6", "type": "concept", "title": "Entitlement risk and schedule impact"}], "description": "Students distinguish land-use approvals from building-code approvals."}, {"id": "262b8f01-2227-499a-96a2-2305521abb8a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Site and environmental constraints", "children": [{"id": "87a91404-9559-432b-be57-3e6268c56f2f", "type": "concept", "title": "Flood zones, coastal zones, wildfire interface, seismic zones, wetlands, stormwater, easements, and rights-of-way"}, {"id": "9c0c4a5c-5b91-4791-8f47-43a2af6d2bb8", "type": "concept", "title": "Historic districts, overlay zones, and neighborhood review"}, {"id": "3f0206dc-c188-447f-8973-29fcdec0ef76", "type": "concept", "title": "Utility, access, emergency vehicle, and fire apparatus constraints"}], "description": "The gist is to identify external controls before design commitments harden."}, {"id": "462cd9e6-e3a2-4732-9bd6-079652974986", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Resilience and local regulation", "children": [{"id": "83ade896-667a-4ae9-b022-37ba29a86efe", "type": "concept", "title": "Floodproofing, freeboard, defensible space, heat, storm shelter, and backup power"}, {"id": "3f352d1b-687e-4005-a768-7bfd11524421", "type": "concept", "title": "Local sustainability ordinances and benchmarking"}, {"id": "d4d8fc4e-44fb-4ba8-a98a-fb4bcf607481", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with civil, landscape, and environmental consultants"}], "description": "Students learn how local regulation responds to climate and hazard conditions."}]}, {"id": "2bb7da85-8e53-4cdf-99b0-746aaa3e140e", "type": "topic", "title": "Existing Buildings, Alterations, Adaptive Reuse, Historic Preservation, Change of Occupancy, and IEBC Strategy", "description": "Students learn how existing-building work differs from new construction and how code strategy can enable safe, feasible reuse without erasing building history.", "children": [{"id": "a28a724e-c7b7-4d3d-bf83-a6be3f728b95", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Existing-building code paths", "children": [{"id": "a05d2d0b-49a0-436d-b633-593c18e28f0d", "type": "concept", "title": "IEBC work area, prescriptive, performance, and compliance methods"}, {"id": "fe3d1431-3fa2-406d-8e5c-283f01bbf73d", "type": "concept", "title": "Repairs, alterations, additions, relocation, and change of occupancy"}, {"id": "61e73890-373f-445e-8e24-7551c1ad075f", "type": "concept", "title": "Trigger points and scoping decisions"}], "description": "Students learn that renovation is not simply new-construction code applied backwards."}, {"id": "b587a0ef-81e0-4f11-b2d8-450a175508ba", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Adaptive reuse strategy", "children": [{"id": "6c2edbad-d1e5-4b38-bee1-135fa8d67e0b", "type": "concept", "title": "Existing conditions surveys, code deficiency matrices, structural and fire/life-safety upgrades"}, {"id": "c85352e7-783b-4a17-9544-1d21bfe9dc8a", "type": "concept", "title": "Egress, accessibility, hazardous materials, and systems replacement"}, {"id": "aef859c9-5645-4434-b574-2fb42f941a7d", "type": "concept", "title": "Phasing, tenant occupation, and constructability"}], "description": "The point is to make reuse safe and feasible."}, {"id": "9f30f8b9-e3b2-4c57-a40d-1251d66ad6b2", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Historic preservation", "children": [{"id": "399a227e-9c8d-48d1-b5d2-c15964834301", "type": "concept", "title": "Historic building provisions, preservation standards, character-defining features, and alternative compliance"}, {"id": "90a64331-4ec5-4362-b081-2c15cbcac22c", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility and life-safety upgrades in historic fabric"}, {"id": "8673b44d-42ee-45d3-b97a-202cf9826261", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with preservation boards and AHJs"}], "description": "Students balance public safety with cultural value."}]}, {"id": "401e8710-2ef1-454a-a31d-0ec25b34d66f", "type": "topic", "title": "Code Analysis Drawings, Life-Safety Plans, Diagrams, Schedules, and Compliance Narratives", "description": "This topic turns code interpretation into architectural documentation: plans, diagrams, tables, narratives, and reviewer-ready evidence.", "children": [{"id": "23303653-a06c-4519-850c-a8d0554caa94", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Code sheets", "children": [{"id": "132e1104-ec25-4e5b-90f2-b6463f42f06d", "type": "concept", "title": "Applicable codes, edition, jurisdiction, occupancy, construction type, area, height, sprinklers, and special inspections"}, {"id": "f215755a-d82c-463f-b195-04cd2b7f7520", "type": "concept", "title": "Code summary sheets and project data"}, {"id": "7ae9acb9-9151-42b6-97af-6668909271f1", "type": "concept", "title": "Assumptions, exceptions, and alternates"}], "description": "Students learn what reviewers need to see quickly."}, {"id": "a2af39c2-b82e-47f6-ba97-ad78628863b6", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Life-safety drawings", "children": [{"id": "2f8286c1-07cb-4e4e-b2cf-589021f6f893", "type": "concept", "title": "Occupant-load plans, egress paths, exit widths, travel distances, rated walls, smoke barriers, and fire extinguishers"}, {"id": "e05660b9-92b2-4f02-8266-c048ce0bc4a0", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility diagrams and plumbing fixture counts"}, {"id": "e0f2ed1c-9fbe-451e-ac95-106aa9605a16", "type": "concept", "title": "Graphic hierarchy and reviewer legibility"}], "description": "The gist is to make compliance visible."}, {"id": "6cbc0b6c-a5d9-4c5a-bdf5-570b6d01fea5", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Narratives and matrices", "children": [{"id": "9de7abce-fa07-411a-a7bf-0413e8f36af6", "type": "concept", "title": "Compliance narratives, code modification requests, alternate means and methods, and variance support"}, {"id": "68081cdd-7ca9-41da-bb46-feb5c7f54ca0", "type": "concept", "title": "Comment response logs and revision clouds"}, {"id": "384974ef-9525-4877-a607-b94475e61ddf", "type": "concept", "title": "Clear citation and evidence standards"}], "description": "Students practice written code reasoning."}]}, {"id": "8af55417-2179-48af-95f7-ac9e34511857", "type": "topic", "title": "Plan Review, Permitting, Inspections, Deferred Submittals, Special Inspections, and AHJ Communication", "description": "Students learn the regulatory workflow from pre-application research through permit submission, comments, revisions, inspection, and closeout.", "children": [{"id": "355a1c2b-8303-429e-b1e9-d9a79d51b40f", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Permit workflow", "children": [{"id": "1fb8baf0-1a53-4dc7-addc-d439d661a1ea", "type": "concept", "title": "Pre-submittal meetings, permit applications, drawing completeness, fees, and intake checks"}, {"id": "d63bf8a6-3c52-4c14-bf3b-9929fe62b454", "type": "concept", "title": "Plan review cycles, comments, resubmittals, approvals, and permits"}, {"id": "ca352e39-279e-46a7-84ef-cd061e9ea941", "type": "concept", "title": "Fast-track, phased, foundation-only, and tenant permits"}], "description": "Students learn how projects move through review."}, {"id": "6968f9df-657e-481b-a1fd-11ec05e012f1", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Construction-phase compliance", "children": [{"id": "3ecf3028-e7cd-42a2-a983-60138398ecc7", "type": "concept", "title": "Inspections, special inspections, deferred submittals, shop drawings, substitutions, and field changes"}, {"id": "b056a46b-7488-4ec8-965d-79016c64d4de", "type": "concept", "title": "Requests for information, ASIs, CCDs, and change orders"}, {"id": "99c608f8-2583-47aa-9040-580271ed04b3", "type": "concept", "title": "Certificate of occupancy and closeout"}], "description": "The point is to keep code decisions alive after permit approval."}, {"id": "2c25f33e-6a9a-4c3d-adcc-0bf72ea2c18a", "type": "subtopic", "title": "AHJ communication", "children": [{"id": "e5061e7e-1f6d-4ca9-bc28-c456aa3b65b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Meeting agendas, code questions, written interpretations, and decision logs"}, {"id": "5320e545-a34a-4049-a760-9834fe0b2d51", "type": "concept", "title": "Respectful negotiation and documentation"}, {"id": "40496df5-9f04-4922-abc3-3ebae06de8e6", "type": "concept", "title": "When to escalate to consultants, attorneys, or senior leadership"}], "description": "Students learn professional communication with reviewers."}]}, {"id": "3690c5c0-b874-417a-a513-b6bb72a3b3c7", "type": "topic", "title": "Risk, Ethics, Professional Responsibility, Equity, and Code Judgment", "description": "This module addresses the architect's responsibility to protect public welfare while navigating ambiguity, minimum requirements, professional liability, cost pressure, and equitable access.", "children": [{"id": "70232667-eeb9-4078-8afe-31702554a1df", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Public welfare duty", "children": [{"id": "d7f1f73b-d139-4c3b-a9f0-f74713140bd7", "type": "concept", "title": "Health, safety, welfare, licensure, standard of care, and responsible control"}, {"id": "ac297f3b-db79-46d1-bf1c-b5675d448183", "type": "concept", "title": "Minimum code versus foreseeable risk"}, {"id": "e1b1890d-f977-4375-8fe9-d707b2356c8b", "type": "concept", "title": "Documentation and decision accountability"}], "description": "Students connect codes to professional ethics."}, {"id": "51b7b0a4-9eed-46bc-985b-141c499c2c21", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Ambiguity and judgment", "children": [{"id": "062f9673-e7c4-4753-b1b5-f4718b3f6c02", "type": "concept", "title": "Conflicting provisions, unclear definitions, alternative interpretations, and local practices"}, {"id": "683148c4-7312-400f-b1c9-e52695089bc2", "type": "concept", "title": "Appeals, equivalencies, and performance-based approaches"}, {"id": "311dd005-2e1a-4433-aa0e-2cb9cf1d538a", "type": "concept", "title": "Risk registers and peer review"}], "description": "The gist is to manage uncertain code situations without pretending they are simple."}, {"id": "ceb9279d-8088-4d74-a2fc-04baa1caa604", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Equity and access", "children": [{"id": "6dd609b0-fcf7-42ef-8615-4511162a3f76", "type": "concept", "title": "Accessibility, affordability, housing supply, climate resilience, and public safety tradeoffs"}, {"id": "7592248e-f728-4249-987b-d09524fe0225", "type": "concept", "title": "Unintended consequences of regulation"}, {"id": "7d98a27d-d87e-4595-bd89-da45b6849e09", "type": "concept", "title": "Ethical client advising and user-centered code strategy"}], "description": "Students learn that compliance choices affect real users."}]}, {"id": "d915b53d-af0a-47ac-aeab-0aa7ae0c15ee", "type": "topic", "title": "Applied Code Integration Capstone: Code-Compliant Design, Redlines, Review Response, and Final Package", "description": "Students synthesize the course by applying code analysis to a real or studio project and producing a professional review package with reasoning, drawings, and revisions.", "children": [{"id": "a4c9f1f3-45c5-4fed-be20-ea9cc01b5f90", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Project selection and baseline analysis", "children": [{"id": "b78f4cd5-7f39-4a6c-a1e7-e15651c561b7", "type": "concept", "title": "Jurisdiction, adopted codes, occupancy, construction type, site constraints, and project scope"}, {"id": "d96bcbfd-4157-4f3a-b680-b87f79e3f2b6", "type": "concept", "title": "Existing or new building assumptions"}, {"id": "f4dd56ba-5585-4fc7-ba74-22446885bbbd", "type": "concept", "title": "Initial code-risk memo"}], "description": "Students define a realistic building or studio project for applied code work."}, {"id": "0837bc99-80c8-42f3-a71c-fcf901aedcdc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Design integration", "children": [{"id": "d1ecc8ca-6817-4fcd-81fc-9f1338647d80", "type": "concept", "title": "Egress layout, accessibility route, rated assemblies, fixture counts, energy approach, and zoning constraints"}, {"id": "2b91f387-16b6-4a3b-a577-86fa49273d69", "type": "concept", "title": "Coordination with structure, envelope, MEP, and fire protection"}, {"id": "37eb6a3f-d896-4a29-8c25-c0b35c6d05f4", "type": "concept", "title": "Redlines and design alternatives"}], "description": "The point is to revise design from code evidence rather than bolt compliance on at the end."}, {"id": "559797c5-6a35-4ad3-ad5e-0a428d956cbc", "type": "subtopic", "title": "Final package", "children": [{"id": "70874ad1-32bb-4588-a667-621b9d37319f", "type": "concept", "title": "Code summary, life-safety plan, accessibility audit, egress calculations, zoning matrix, and permit narrative"}, {"id": "3b4334f2-4c39-4dc9-94a5-c92a9c7b417d", "type": "concept", "title": "Reviewer comment response simulation"}, {"id": "834e8082-621f-43d7-b6bd-f9c594a2202a", "type": "concept", "title": "Reflection on judgment, ethics, and lessons learned"}], "description": "Students produce professional deliverables."}]}], "dbBacked": true}$bcdrjson$::jsonb,
